1785294105184967	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyhgI		item.update	bn-3dl	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nContinuum is a verification-native environment for concurrent/distributed Rust (asupersync runtime, Lean 4 proofs, TLA+ corpus oracle harness). The authoritative plan is the Revision 3 dossier at notes/plan/ — plan.md plus docs/00–55, rfcs/0001–0040, adr/0001–0052, research notes, JSON schemas, executed Python spikes, and a mechanical validator (tools/validate_dossier.py, currently: status=pass, program_status=ready, execution_model=autonomous-agent-swarm). The product thesis (plan §0.1–0.2): the product IS the proof-guided loop — protected intent → immutable snapshot → typed verification task → exact failure → bounded Context Pack → causal debugger → repair transaction → semantic/intent diff → neighborhood/mutation challenge → independent evidence → promotion receipt.\n\n# Goals\nShip Continuum 1.0 by closing release gates G0–G10 (plan §22, docs/52) through six gate-driven phases A–F (plan §21). G10 = real adoption: two materially different real systems replace bespoke DST + separate TLA+ workflow.\n\n# Structure of this bone graph\n- Child goals: Phase A–F (one per plan §21 phase) plus the §24.5 frontier-lane ratification register.\n- Phase goals are dependency-chained A→B→C→D→E→F (gate-driven sequencing, plan §21.1).\n- PR-level tasks (START_HERE_IMPLEMENTATION.md, PR 0–30 + lettered inserts 4a/15a/15b/22a/25a/26a/27a/27b) live under their owning phase. The PR sequence interleaves phases BY DESIGN — a phase closes when its gates close, regardless of PR ordinal; a PR advancing an already-closed gate is hardening, not reopening.\n- True blockers (PR 0→PR 5, PR 22a→PR 23, register ratification→phase opening PRs, etc.) are wired as bn deps, not implied by numbering.\n\n# Governing doctrine (plan §26, §21.1, START_HERE)\nClose the smallest complete trustworthy loop before broadening the verifier. No polished web UI, general LLM coordinator, distributed search, or full CML surface before the loop closes. Phases are gate-driven, not time-driven; each phase is BLOCKED until its §21.1 owner/team row is filled (a merge requirement of the phase's opening PR). A misleading assurance result, replay failure, stale receipt, hidden intent change, or unauthorized promotion is a release blocker at every gate.\n\n# How to navigate the source of truth\n- plan.md — the map (2700+ lines); §21 phases, §22 gates, §24 kill criteria, §24.5 research-lane register, §25 spec-debt ledger.\n- notes/START_HERE_IMPLEMENTATION.md — PR-by-PR implementation sequence with exit conditions (bone descriptions quote these).\n- docs/52_RELEASE_GATES_REV3.md — normative gate text (kept bullet-identical with plan §22 by the validator).\n- notes/G0_SPIKE_MATRIX.md — falsification matrix DX-01..15; freeze-blocking subset DX-01–03, 10, 12, 13, 14.\n- Where plan prose and an RFC disagree, the RFC is corrected and becomes normative (plan §25)."}	blake3:83Ef68gSocU3cNE335sMunI2bHyNu6EE3HK7LjDhhTc
1785294105209117	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyhwI		item.update	bn-3dl	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nContinuum is a verification-native environment for concurrent/distributed Rust (asupersync runtime, Lean 4 proofs, TLA+ corpus oracle harness). The authoritative plan is the Revision 3 dossier at notes/plan/ — plan.md plus docs/00–55, rfcs/0001–0040, adr/0001–0052, research notes, JSON schemas, executed Python spikes, and a mechanical validator (tools/validate_dossier.py, currently: status=pass, program_status=ready, execution_model=autonomous-agent-swarm). The product thesis (plan §0.1–0.2): the product IS the proof-guided loop — protected intent → immutable snapshot → typed verification task → exact failure → bounded Context Pack → causal debugger → repair transaction → semantic/intent diff → neighborhood/mutation challenge → independent evidence → promotion receipt.\n\n# Goals\nShip Continuum 1.0 by closing release gates G0–G10 (plan §22, docs/52) through six gate-driven phases A–F (plan §21). G10 = real adoption: two materially different real systems replace bespoke DST + separate TLA+ workflow.\n\n# Structure of this bone graph\n- Child goals: Phase A–F (one per plan §21 phase) plus the §24.5 frontier-lane ratification register.\n- Phase goals are dependency-chained A→B→C→D→E→F (gate-driven sequencing, plan §21.1).\n- PR-level tasks (START_HERE_IMPLEMENTATION.md, PR 0–30 + lettered inserts 4a/15a/15b/22a/25a/26a/27a/27b) live under their owning phase. The PR sequence interleaves phases BY DESIGN — a phase closes when its gates close, regardless of PR ordinal; a PR advancing an already-closed gate is hardening, not reopening.\n- True blockers (PR 0→PR 5, PR 22a→PR 23, register ratification→phase opening PRs, etc.) are wired as bn deps, not implied by numbering.\n\n# Governing doctrine (plan §26, §21.1, START_HERE)\nClose the smallest complete trustworthy loop before broadening the verifier. No polished web UI, general LLM coordinator, distributed search, or full CML surface before the loop closes. Phases are gate-driven, not time-driven; the elastic autonomous-agent swarm takes dependency-ready bones in parallel, while integration evidence, authority boundaries, and phase exits remain fail-closed. A misleading assurance result, replay failure, stale receipt, hidden intent change, or unauthorized promotion is a release blocker at every gate.\n\n# How to navigate the source of truth\n- plan.md — the map (2700+ lines); §21 phases, §22 gates, §24 kill criteria, §24.5 research-lane register, §25 spec-debt ledger.\n- notes/START_HERE_IMPLEMENTATION.md — PR-by-PR implementation sequence with exit conditions (bone descriptions quote these).\n- docs/52_RELEASE_GATES_REV3.md — normative gate text (kept bullet-identical with plan §22 by the validator).\n- notes/G0_SPIKE_MATRIX.md — falsification matrix DX-01..15; freeze-blocking subset DX-01–03, 10, 12, 13, 14.\n- Where plan prose and an RFC disagree, the RFC is corrected and becomes normative (plan §25)."}	blake3:5PX-8OsOIYWv5C_nGgRr9o2pEPjcfEpFKSZicxiedUg
1785294105229278	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyiAI		item.update	bn-19p	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nPhase A (plan §21) builds the trust spine: everything the rest of the program stands on. It owns gates G0 (falsification, freeze-blocking subset), G1 (workbench identity and lifecycle), and G2 (agent-computer interface) per the phase↔gate table (plan §22, docs/52). Opening PR: PR 0 (plan §21.1). Contains PRs 0–13 plus the PR 4a Lean band.\n\n# Goals (plan §21 Phase A deliverables)\n- Immutable workspace and intent schemas; native daemon protocol; task/continuation lifecycle; evidence graph; Context Pack v0; semantic diff v0.\n- Executable finite reference engine and certificates from Revision 2.\n- The four continuum-kernel-* crates under the docs/03 covenant (<15,000 non-test lines, no async, no unsafe, serialization boundary between engines and kernel) — phase-normative, not only a PR 9 annotation.\n- Agent protocol spike parity; Lean environment pinned, seed modules kernel-checked, T0/T1 theorems with no `sorry` and empty axiom manifests (RFC 0012 ladder, ADR-0035).\n\n# Exit criteria (verbatim intent, plan §21)\nDie Hard and Dining Philosophers check through native API, CLI, and an agent client with identical artifacts; the ACI ablation shows the typed surface beats disciplined shell use on success and cost, or the protocol is redesigned before freeze (G0-DX-10); the prompt-injection corpus cannot trigger privileged operations (G2); continuation resume validates epochs and inputs (G1).\n\n# G0 staging (plan §22)\nThe freeze-blocking subset DX-01–03, 10, 12, 13, 14 must close in Phase A. DX-01/02/03/12 already carry finite-spike evidence (artifact shapes); DX-13 closes via PR 2, DX-14 via PR 6, DX-10 via PR 10. An unexecuted or failed item in this subset blocks interface freeze.\n\n# Governance\nPhase A is ready for autonomous-agent dispatch through dependency-ready bones; PR 0 has no staffing prerequisite. Phase A also resolves: product license (permissive, asupersync/solver-compatible), corpus per-family redistribution audit, and the ADR-0029 rule that foreign oracle tooling never ships in release binaries.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nEvery later phase consumes Phase A's identities (snapshots, intents, handles, epochs), its protocol, and its trusted kernel. Getting the fail-closed spine right first is the plan's core doctrine: close the smallest trustworthy loop before broadening the verifier."}	blake3:4bYu8HViJjRIR2p2Z5XtzJV_hgMeYWz4mVpuYUgjFLw
1785294105251482	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyiQI		item.update	bn-3zt	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nPhase B (plan §21) closes the first end-to-end loop on real code: a real asupersync failure is diagnosed and repaired with a promotion receipt. Owns gates G3 (intent integrity) and G4 (causal debugging and real repair). Opening PR: PR 14 (asupersync semantic journal). Contains PRs 14–22a (minus 15b, which delivers Phase C's G5 CML-stability deliverable).\n\n# Goals (plan §21 Phase B deliverables)\n- Asupersync semantic adapter: one adapter crate; supported upstream window N and N−1 covered by a conformance corpus in CI against upstream pre-releases; upstream breaking release opens a scheduled adapter epoch (§4.6) visible in envelopes; explicit semantic-hook contract; the model core never depends on asupersync.\n- CML core-fragment parser and elaborator (Finite fragment; PR 15a).\n- Storage/network/process packs; replicated register with mutants; causal minimizer; debugger v0; repair transactions; exact and neighboring replay.\n- The incremental-engine ADR (build-vs-adopt, salsa-derived vs custom) with reuse-edge spike — Phase C is BLOCKED until this ADR exists (PR 22a).\n\n# Exit criteria (plan §21)\nAn agent fixes ack-before-durable without changing intent and produces a promotion receipt under the Phase B gate profile. Gate profiles are phase-staged: §8.2 gates 1–8 and 11–12 enter in Phase B; gate 10 in Phase C; gate 9 in Phase D. Receipts name their profile and list not-yet-enforced gates as NotYetEnforced — never as passed.\n\n# Governance\nPhase B opens after Phase A exit acceptance and its explicit Bones prerequisites; no staffing assignment is required. Re-homed G0 items landing here: DX-04 (causal debugger → G4), DX-06 (neighborhood/mutation → G4, PR 21).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThis is the first demonstration of the product theorem on real code — plan §25's \"brutally concrete\" demo (200-event failure → 4-event Context Pack → repair → receipt) runs on Phase B machinery."}	blake3:nPHeJAgpADLIJZoBJUxudvmumzlV3c-TIxWnNanAEcA
1785294105271422	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyigI		item.update	bn-1fd	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nPhase C (plan §21) makes the loop interactive and trustworthy under reuse: incrementality, editor integrations, corpus at bounded parity, and adoption top-of-funnel. Owns gate G5 (incremental trust). Opening PR: PR 23 (incremental query database) — PR 22a precedes it but is Phase B's ADR deliverable. Contains PRs 15b, 23–27b.\n\n# Goals (plan §21 Phase C deliverables)\n- Incremental semantic database; Incremental Parity Audit (§9.5); LSP/DAP/SARIF adapters.\n- Proof Context Pack format and compiler plumbing (proof-worker efficacy criterion is G6's, evaluated in Phase D).\n- Read-only tokio observation lane: envelopes capped at Observed, every claim dimension Unsupported — adoption top-of-funnel making no controlled-semantics claim.\n- Wave 0/1 corpus (29 families, pinned tlaplus/Examples@91c22ea…) at min(required, P2); P3/P4 raises deferred to Phase D.\n- CML language stability: normalized semantic AST frozen before surface syntax; formatter and migration tool (PR 15b) ship before syntax stability (docs/11 §14).\n- Formative usability sessions (~5 participants per §13.1 persona) each phase from C onward, feeding docs/34 — distinct from and non-binding on the summative G8 study.\n\n# Exit criteria (plan §21)\nEdit/check/explain loop meets the docs/34 latency table (p50/p95, gate-normative for G5, including the explain-interaction row) on the reference workload; incremental results trustworthy under differential audit; reduction engines show zero reachability mismatch on the no-reduction corpus with certified claims falling back to unreduced baseline (docs/08 R04); semantic artifacts byte-identical across the docs/19 determinism matrix. G5 latency is measured with a saturating background swarm present (plan §4.1, docs/34). Evidence queries and context compilation meet docs/34 targets at ≥10^7 evidence nodes.\n\n# Governance\nPhase C opens after Phase B exit acceptance and its explicit Bones prerequisites; the PR 22a incremental-engine ADR remains a hard technical prerequisite. Corpus release checkpoint 0.1 closes at Phase C entry.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nInteractive latency and trustworthy reuse are what make the verifier a daily tool instead of a batch oracle; the tokio observed lane is the beachhead for Phase F adoption."}	blake3:9EThO4nJXx5jbUpxT_BIBB3E4_S3tEfnM6zeeCf1pV0
1785294105289321	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyiwI		item.update	bn-1db	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nPhase D (plan §21) brings the Lean proof service online and extends verification to fairness/liveness, raising corpus parity to full requirements. Owns gate G6 (proof service). Opening PR: PR 28 (Lean proof service). The Lean environment itself was pinned in Phase A (PR 4a) — PR 28 is the service.\n\n# Goals (plan §21 Phase D deliverables)\n- Lean theorem/certificate pipeline; fairness/liveness debugger; invariant/ranking synthesis; proof repair service; refinement receipts.\n- Self-application: a CML model of continuumd's task/continuation lifecycle and INV-017 publication protocol, bound to the daemon implementation via §16 correspondence machinery, checked in CI from Phase D onward; a daemon concurrency defect found this way is recorded as G4-class evidence of product value.\n- Wave 0/1 families raised from the Phase C P2 cap to full required parity (14 at P4, 3 at P3); corpus Waves 2–3 at required parity; corpus checkpoint 0.2 closes in Phase D.\n\n# Exit criteria (plan §21)\nOne nontrivial corpus protocol has safety AND liveness evidence plus real-code refinement, produced by a proof service that holds G6: pinned Lean environment, per-request isolation and cancellation, theorem and axiom manifests on every receipt, agent proof repair accepted only by the kernel.\n\n# Governance\nPhase D opens after Phase C exit acceptance and its explicit Bones prerequisites; no staffing assignment is required. Re-homed G0 items: DX-08 (lens ambiguity → G6), DX-11 (proof-service isolation → G6, PR 28). §8.2 gate 9 (certificate/proof rebuild) enters the promotion profile here.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nProof-backed evidence is the top of the assurance ladder; self-application (Continuum verifying continuumd) is both dogfooding and the strongest possible product argument."}	blake3:Iu8DyNawc4AHgnODTmqFb1xgSRC6DR5f0gK9jcSEEHM
1785294105309259	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyjAI		item.update	bn-4rm	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nPhase E (plan §21) delivers Continuum Forge — synthesis under verification. Owns gate G7. Opening PR: PR 29 (Forge finite CEGIS v0). Forge is deliberately last among the engine phases: it must not be imported by the verifier (START_HERE dependency islands), and it multiplies a loop that must already be trustworthy.\n\n# Goals (plan §21 Phase E deliverables)\n- Typed holes and grammars; CEGIS/interpretation reduction; quality-diversity archive; co-synthesis of algorithm/invariant/ranking/abstraction; materialization to asupersync skeleton.\n- Authoring the expanded docs/48 G8 preregistration (workflows, cohorts, baseline comparator, instruments, pass thresholds) — published before the Phase F summative study (plan §21.1; instruments authored under PR 27b).\n- Corpus checkpoint 0.5 (Waves 0–2 at required parity plus ≥5 named P5 exemplar families in validated-examples.csv) closes in Phase E.\n\n# Exit criteria (plan §21)\nForge rediscovers known solutions through typed holes and finite CEGIS, produces at least one behaviorally novel candidate independently verified within the declared envelope, materializes model/Rust skeleton/proof obligations for a promoted candidate, and reports unrealizability as distinct from unknown.\n\n# Governance\nPhase E opens after Phase D exit acceptance and its explicit Bones prerequisites; no staffing assignment is required. Re-homed G0 item: DX-07 (Forge non-vacuity → G7). Register rows for Forge (research/29, research/30) and explanation science (research/34) must ratify before Phase E opens.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nForge closes the full product loop of plan §25's demonstration — from intent through invention and proof: after the repair demo, Forge must solve the same acknowledgement-guard hole from scratch (ack_guard = synced)."}	blake3:68LduUphckGi6g-lFj01dt2lYmJBN6MN_jsc5eGoV88
1785294121525597	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyjQI		item.update	bn-2hm	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nPhase A's designated opening PR (plan §21.1) — documentation and decisions, not code. The Revision 3 RFCs started as summaries; review 5 already absorbed large plan sections into RFCs 0026/0027/0030/0032/0037/0038, but full specification form (field types, enums, classification lattices, IDL, versioning, RFC-2119 language) and the remaining validator-tracked spec debt are this PR. Child tasks cover each open debt item (SD-01/07/08/09/10) and the program decisions.\n\n# Why this comes first\nInterface freeze is fail-closed: PR 5 (native protocol kernel) may not merge before PR 0 closes, so every wire-visible decision is settled on paper before any interface exists to ossify. Where plan prose and RFC disagree, the RFC is corrected and becomes NORMATIVE — the plan is a map, not the spec (plan §25).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria (START_HERE PR 0 exit)\n- [ ] RFCs 0026, 0027, 0028, 0030, 0031, 0032, 0037 are normative specifications (field types, enums, lattices, IDL, versioning, RFC-2119 language), each reconciled one-to-one with its plan section.\n- [ ] The pre-freeze open-debt set (plan §25, validator check_spec_debt) reads EMPTY in validation-results.json — not only the seven RFC expansions.\n- [ ] Product license decided (permissive, compatible with asupersync and solver adapters, §21.1).\n- [ ] Corpus per-family redistribution audit exists (required before any public benchmark release).\n- [ ] ADR-0029 rule recorded: foreign oracle tooling (TLC, Apalache, solvers) never ships in release binaries.\n\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nEvery fail-closed property later phases rely on (typed errors, epochs, authority splits, redaction) is specified here before code exists to violate it."}	blake3:QHdJYOFYfR0A-7xZdsaNkn_88UH4yCDfEPIVsszmVV8
1785294121771370	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyjgI		item.update	bn-2en	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nPhase B's opening PR (plan §21.1): instrument asupersync's narrow primitives to emit canonical semantic events — the raw material for CIR, minimization, and the debugger. START_HERE PR 14.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nJournal task/region lifecycle; reserve/commit/abort; cancellation phases; obligations; virtual time; channel communication. One adapter crate; supported upstream window N and N−1 covered by the adapter conformance corpus in CI against upstream pre-releases; an upstream breaking release opens a scheduled adapter epoch (§4.6) visible in envelopes; explicit semantic-hook contract; the model core never depends on asupersync (dependency-island rule).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Identical controlled choice logs produce canonical identical semantic events.\n\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nEverything G4 promises (causal debugging of REAL code) starts from a trustworthy semantic journal; canonicality here is what makes replay exact."}	blake3:hbFQe-1lg4QjTev4gRlfjDkDKnrw0Z0HnQ-g9TTcPUA
1785294121924125	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyjwI		item.update	bn-z2l	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nPhase C's opening PR (plan §21.1): the §9 incremental semantic database, built or adopted per the PR 22a ADR (hard dep — PR 23 may not merge before PR 22a). START_HERE PR 23.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nContent-addressed queries for parsing, model construction, property automata, exploration, context, and diff. Classify reuse edges as Exact/Validated/Conservative/Experimental.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Property-only edit does not rebuild unrelated extraction.\n- [ ] Model action edit invalidates the reachable graph and dependent context (invalidation cone matches declared dependency classes).\n\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nInteractive latency (G5, docs/34 table) is impossible without trustworthy reuse; the four edge classes are how reuse stays honest (mismatches quarantine the CLASS, not the world — plan §9.5)."}	blake3:1ZZE5mjl4la_F6y_-CEz67h7-mawDpBPAEE2OX06Ew0
1785294122069519	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEykAI		item.update	bn-lb8	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nPhase D's opening PR (plan §21.1): the proof SERVICE over the environment pinned in PR 4a. Closes re-homed G0-DX-11 (proof-service isolation: concurrent Lean epochs, malicious inputs, cancellation → deterministic isolated results and resource bounds). START_HERE PR 28.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nPer-request isolation and cancellation; goals; diagnostics; receipts with theorem/axiom manifests; relevant-context extraction consuming the Phase C proof-pack format. (Lean pinning and T0/T1 are PR 4a, NOT here.)\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Concurrent requests across Lean epochs are isolated and cancellable with deterministic results.\n- [ ] Every proof receipt carries theorem and axiom manifests with zero unapproved axioms.\n\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nG6's doctrine — agent proof repair accepted ONLY by kernel — needs an isolated, cancellable service boundary; failure consequence per the matrix was \"no remote proof lane\"."}	blake3:W0Vz9FtS1M44OPLyfcvPS9xshIjXboGadZagRtGE7-I
1785294122252378	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEykQI		item.update	bn-3ix	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nPhase E's opening PR (plan §21.1): Continuum Forge v0 — synthesis under verification, finite and honest. Closes re-homed G0-DX-07 (Forge non-vacuity; artifact shape from the Phase A spike, engine evidence here per plan §22). START_HERE PR 29. Forge may not be imported by the verifier (dependency-island rule).\n\n# Implementation Approach\nTyped holes; finite grammar enumeration; exact counterexample feedback; safety plus progress/non-vacuity objectives; candidate archive; independent candidate verification (candidates are proposals — the ordinary evidence path grades them, Forge grades nothing itself).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The acknowledgement-guard task synthesizes `synced`, rejects never-ack/`false` (vacuous) candidates, and independently verifies the result (plan §25's from-scratch demonstration: ack_guard = synced).\n\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nNon-vacuity is Forge's soul: without a progress objective, synthesis returns the guard that never acknowledges. The spike proved the objective shape; this makes it an engine."}	blake3:Bl1kVwmnD-2QZL56JONARtjl8QU4jca6oVlmX1F8tzA
1785294122371770	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEykgI		item.update	bn-3ij	{"field":"title","value":"Designate Phase F opening PR (§21.1)"}	blake3:m2hxzke57E6K_kl4DGiDu4XHmFWIx-mebu-Dyeo2Arw
1785294122376002	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEykwI		item.update	bn-3ij	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nEvery Phase F deliverable sits in the deliberately deferred set, so its opening PR is the first PR sequenced after PR 30 closes. The designation is an ordering marker for swarm dispatch, not a staffing prerequisite.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The Phase F opening PR is designated in START_HERE swarm execution map.\n- [ ] Its phase-entry and task-level dependency edges are represented in Bones before Phase F work is dispatched.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMakes the least-sequenced phase unambiguous and safely dispatchable without inventing a human resourcing gate."}	blake3:KH-P05wm2Qsq9E0QSZ7bLTHir5NfnbjFMFopme2ldcA
