1785348475389237	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyrgI		item.create	bn-3rqvm	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPR 13 (START_HERE:256) lists eight command families but the graph carried only the exit-evidence Bone, so the CLI itself had no implementable work item. This Bone owns the read/verify path: `continuum snapshot`, `continuum check`, `continuum explain`.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nBuild the three commands over the PR 5 native protocol — the CLI is a client of the same daemon surface agents use, never a second code path. `snapshot` imports/forks/seals a workspace snapshot (PR 3); `check` submits a verification task against an Intent Contract (PR 4) and renders the assurance verdict; `explain` renders a Context Pack (PR 11) projection. Prose output is a projection of the JSON, never a separate truth (plan §3.1).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each command is a thin projection of a native protocol operation — no CLI-only server behavior exists.\n- [ ] `check` renders verdict, assurance, and inconclusive reason from the machine result without re-deriving them.\n- [ ] Golden tests pin JSON, exit codes, and terminal rendering for all three commands.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThis is the first-party dogfooding surface for the implementation team, the plan's declared first users. If prose and JSON can disagree here, INV-003 (no prose-only machine interfaces) is unenforceable.\n","kind":"task","labels":["implementation","req:phase-a","req:pr-13","ux"],"parent":"bn-1xn","size":"m","title":"continuum snapshot/check/explain: core inspection command family"}	blake3:q5BqTNAkCK_cRhDhgAMPpCexTGN-c2K9nKSEnB0muFY
1785348475389365	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyrwI		item.link	bn-2hmk	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-3rqvm"}	blake3:I2RBwIwYO79GPbYA7YFrFKuzGOR2knnXXIIIxedpzkc
1785348475389408	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEysAI		item.link	bn-2ofd	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-3rqvm"}	blake3:yw_Sa-A8Oc9TwZMcoFR38E2X6XAnRtblgjXFs0xGcSM
1785348475467371	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEysQI		item.create	bn-1g7e4	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe second PR 13 command group (START_HERE:256), covering the failure-investigation and promotion path that Phases B–C build out.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n`debug` drives the PR 19 verification debugger over an explicit failure handle; `repair` opens and inspects a PR 20 repair transaction; `evidence show` queries the PR 7 evidence graph by handle. All three take explicit handles — no ambient 'current' session state (INV-002). Commands degrade honestly when the backing service is a Phase A stub: a typed unsupported result, not a crash.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every command takes an explicit handle; there is no implicit session cursor.\n- [ ] Against Phase A services, unimplemented depth returns a valid machine result naming what is unsupported rather than failing opaquely.\n- [ ] Golden tests pin JSON, exit codes, and terminal rendering for all three commands.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThese are the commands a human reaches for when something is wrong; they are also the commands the G8 study measures. Building them on explicit handles now is what lets the Phase C editor and debugger adapters reuse the same semantics.\n","kind":"task","labels":["implementation","req:phase-a","req:pr-13","ux"],"parent":"bn-1xn","size":"m","title":"continuum debug/repair/evidence show: failure and promotion command family"}	blake3:86Rx2GXj72PtHPIN6yMd4s3LpwjRdEUfauYN5oJduFM
1785348475467500	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEysgI		item.link	bn-2hmk	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-1g7e4"}	blake3:Mu_aZC26QxS91kWq1deEA8PaJo5jt7I3nLzwnvZdaVY
1785348475467543	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyswI		item.link	bn-2ofd	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-1g7e4"}	blake3:uALwhDLBjsqRsgMO6fK0MW1kVmHjVrjDhqSS0a2RQ9k
1785348475543795	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEytAI		item.create	bn-3tz60	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe third PR 13 command group (START_HERE:256): the Context Pack expansion handle surface and the task lifecycle surface that PR 6 makes cancel-correct.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n`context expand` follows a PR 11 expansion handle to retrieve omitted detail and prints the omission manifest. `task status|resume|cancel` drives the PR 6 lifecycle, including validating a continuation's epochs and inputs before resume.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `context expand` surfaces the omission manifest (INV-007) alongside the expanded slice.\n- [ ] `task resume` refuses a continuation whose epoch or inputs no longer validate, with a typed reason.\n- [ ] `task cancel` at any instrumented phase leaves either a valid continuation or a clean absence — the PR 6 contract, exercised from the CLI.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nOmission transparency and cancel-correctness are invariants, not features. A human surface that hides either one would let the team ship a daemon that violates them without noticing.\n","kind":"task","labels":["implementation","req:phase-a","req:pr-13","ux"],"parent":"bn-1xn","size":"s","title":"continuum context expand and task status/resume/cancel: continuation command family"}	blake3:28pmWoR5r5DlzEZK4d-7EikKT-LUxPEr-OBaEXkNnTA
