1785348475543958	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEytgI		item.link	bn-2ofd	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-3tz60"}	blake3:9kv5J8zhjA5w-GaKeYsC8Vz_UZ6830h61VKsfz6x4pQ
1785348475626433	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEytwI		item.create	bn-ybh1z	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPR 13's exit criterion is 'golden tests pin JSON, exit codes, and concise terminal output' (START_HERE:256). That contract is cross-cutting across every command and needs one owner rather than being re-invented per command.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nDefine one output contract: `--json` emits the machine result verbatim; the terminal rendering is a pure function of that JSON; a documented exit-code taxonomy distinguishes success, refuted, inconclusive, and operational failure. No critical workflow may require color or a rendered graph — the G8-06 accessibility bullet is designed in here, not retrofitted in Phase F.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Terminal output is generated from the JSON result by a pure renderer; a golden test asserts the two cannot diverge.\n- [ ] Exit codes distinguish refuted from inconclusive from operational failure, and are documented.\n- [ ] Every command's critical path is complete with color disabled and no graph rendering (feeds G8-06).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nINV-003 and G8-06 both live or die on this contract. Owning it as one Bone means the accessibility gate has an implementation home in Phase A instead of an audit finding in Phase F.\n","kind":"task","labels":["implementation","req:phase-a","req:pr-13","ux"],"parent":"bn-1xn","size":"m","title":"CLI output contract: --json projection, exit-code taxonomy, and non-color rendering"}	blake3:RzNkTMDDXEyDq-TJgt5qhjR31p1_EKljo8AO_emIaJo
1785348475626553	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyuAI		item.link	bn-2hmk	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-ybh1z"}	blake3:6w2S1xtVkWZPQh7Vlcs7hnZOjZrX3q0czT7ZsejmJIU
1785348475626598	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyuQI		item.link	bn-2ofd	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-ybh1z"}	blake3:F0Jod1FZ3VhEvS1JwVTWE9s1L1KOXBI9_bqetgjj5lk
1785348475698504	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyugI		item.create	bn-3km4z	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPR 18 (START_HERE:343) specifies four reduction techniques; the graph carried only the exit-evidence Bone. This Bone owns the two structural passes that do the bulk of the shrinking.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nImplement delta-debugging-style event deletion over the semantic event trace, then causal-closure reduction that keeps an event only if it is reachable in the happens-before relation from the failure. Both passes operate on the PR 17 CIR semantic events, not on raw logs.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Deletion reduces the ack-before-sync failure trace substantially and terminates deterministically.\n- [ ] Causal closure never drops an event on a happens-before path to the failure.\n- [ ] Both passes are deterministic: identical input traces produce byte-identical cores (INV-006).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThis is the engine behind the Context Pack reduction headline. Determinism here is what makes the reduced core citable as evidence rather than a lossy summary.\n","kind":"task","labels":["debugger","implementation","req:phase-b","req:pr-18"],"parent":"bn-8pr","size":"m","title":"Deletion and causal-closure reduction over failing traces"}	blake3:lVhbRSaYk1nhIxbx3B3qcF9rzwN98nlhf9CTObVT9so
1785348475698630	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyuwI		item.link	bn-2nh6	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-3km4z"}	blake3:P4y33wUi3zMBfFrib9dxWZhTlhOa6vGtbmW_wmDZvy0
1785348475698674	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyvAI		item.link	bn-188z9	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-3km4z"}	blake3:oZgS56rzwsfqnWKQBDbOOI2UNYkL4M-W6MzsapbhStQ
1785348475737304	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyvQI		item.link	bn-3km4z	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-19p"}	blake3:r_vgvg77DlBCEWOMSGeeWywKC6wuX55E0qEW7Ev882c
1785348475818383	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyvgI		item.create	bn-25z9o	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe remaining two PR 18 reduction dimensions (START_HERE:343): reduce the number of participating owners/processes, the injected faults, and the value domains, not just the event count.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nImplement owner reduction (fewer participants reproducing the same failure), fault reduction (fewer injected faults), and value reduction (smaller/canonical values). Each pass declares which intent properties it must preserve so a reduction cannot silently change what is being verified.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each pass reduces the ack-before-sync case along its dimension while the failure still reproduces.\n- [ ] Every pass declares its preserved property scope; a pass that would change the checked property refuses instead of reducing (INV-013).\n- [ ] Reduction cost is measured and recorded against the research/26 kill signal (≤10% of trace length on real, non-synthetic failures).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe register kill criterion for causal minimization is that minimization cost dominates verification cost; measuring per-dimension cost here is what lets the fallback to 1-minimal delta debugging be a decision rather than a surprise.\n","kind":"task","labels":["debugger","implementation","req:phase-b","req:pr-18"],"parent":"bn-8pr","size":"m","title":"Owner, fault, and value-domain reduction passes"}	blake3:XzbpkBm6TS8Vr0Iof7gVAxrcDEYgnke7-Ba7sDF5ft4
1785348475818514	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyvwI		item.link	bn-2nh6	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-25z9o"}	blake3:Z2UDtfiRWTOHQm6zVR7KQM85uAKdnY3_hGQhLqwg-t0
1785348475818558	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEywAI		item.link	bn-188z9	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-25z9o"}	blake3:cwJndef0r-0rj_hv9z9ZlzbhHwZg4g3X3-g0owxCLtU
1785348475848362	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEywQI		item.link	bn-25z9o	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-19p"}	blake3:U0PhlajV_K4atW4PyI6_c4awikEvxaLmYC5TyIDXmWc
1785348475918703	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEywgI		item.create	bn-5kmuf	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPR 18's exit criterion has two halves and the second is the hard one: the reduced core must not delete the actual causal mechanism (START_HERE:343).\n\n# Implementation Approach\nAfter every reduction, replay the core and validate that it reproduces the same failure through the same causal mechanism — not merely the same verdict. Build the mechanism-preservation check as a first-class validator, and add a negative corpus of reductions that reach the right verdict by the wrong path.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The ack-before-sync core replays to the same failure through the same causal mechanism.\n- [ ] A seeded 'right verdict, wrong mechanism' reduction is rejected by the validator.\n- [ ] Validation runs on every reduction, not as a sampled audit.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nWithout mechanism preservation, minimization produces confident, compact, wrong explanations — precisely the failure mode the plan's honesty invariants exist to prevent.\n","kind":"task","labels":["debugger","implementation","req:phase-b","req:pr-18"],"parent":"bn-8pr","size":"m","title":"Replay validation: reduced cores preserve the causal mechanism"}	blake3:6sSCDezBnljpPrUg275Ri_1XssNBlrRJeh3IM-UT9Ts
