1785348476345393	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy1QI		item.link	bn-r4x4v	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-3zt"}	blake3:O9Sx4zZ6jeSyBjrEIozzgJxH43JPoI3Zh_ZnQgrsBI4
1785348476419977	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy1gI		item.create	bn-sph8k	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPR 25's exit criterion (START_HERE:421) is that unsaved buffer overlays create explicit snapshots and never mutate daemon state implicitly. That is the adapter's foundational design decision and needs its own Bone.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nMap every editor buffer state to an explicit PR 3 workspace snapshot with an editor overlay. The adapter holds snapshot handles; it never writes through to daemon state. Overlay snapshots are garbage-collected on an explicit policy, and a stale overlay produces a typed stale-snapshot error rather than silent recomputation.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every unsaved-buffer analysis runs against an explicit overlay snapshot handle.\n- [ ] No LSP request path mutates daemon state; a test asserts daemon state is unchanged across an editing session.\n- [ ] A stale overlay returns the typed stale-snapshot error (PR 3 IMPL-04 contract).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nEditor integration over mutable daemon state is how verification tools become non-reproducible. Making the overlay explicit keeps every editor-time result citable and replayable.\n","kind":"task","labels":["implementation","req:phase-c","req:pr-25","ux"],"parent":"bn-2yb","size":"m","title":"LSP snapshot overlay model for unsaved buffers"}	blake3:PS1TBofct6rWg28ipC8tN6Cw8iuQJ1AFuzWW2utjYM8
1785348476420101	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy1wI		item.link	bn-239q	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-sph8k"}	blake3:bHeEmOt4dtSR0LlayvRDp7jUvh0Nczow3-Fr5dLR_Sc
