1785348476656978	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy4QI		item.link	bn-ppr6w	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-3zt"}	blake3:R8TCP-lcFYTovLEpxY8q9W2slOU9AcMuQGuSFFFkwyQ
1785348476729053	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy4gI		item.create	bn-55h5m	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPR 26 (START_HERE:439) requires mapping Continuum debugger state onto the standard DAP surface; the graph carried only the exit-evidence Bone.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nMap PR 19 debugger state to DAP: owners/processes to threads, causal positions to frames, model state to scopes and variables, semantic predicates to breakpoints, and causal successor selection to stepping. The mapping is a projection — DAP operations never introduce debugger state that the native surface cannot express.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A VS Code-compatible client can attach to a replicated-register failure and inspect state through standard DAP requests.\n- [ ] Every DAP operation is expressible on the native debugger surface; the adapter holds no private state.\n- [ ] Stepping is deterministic: the same client actions on the same failure handle produce the same sequence (INV-006).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nCausal debugging inside a familiar surface is the adoption wedge for engineers who will never learn a bespoke debugger UI. Keeping the adapter stateless is what stops DAP from becoming a second, weaker semantics.\n","kind":"task","labels":["debugger","implementation","req:phase-c","req:pr-26"],"parent":"bn-21g","size":"m","title":"DAP state mapping: threads, frames, scopes, variables, breakpoints, stepping"}	blake3:oaOy4ATcIUt5PZB2JvKCM_umiu4wEEuVTLc8fc1RpRQ
