1785348476873758	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy6QI		item.link	bn-2zzs0	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-3zt"}	blake3:IypcPq5LnBQeuWL7X999p5ptQhx97__kLsRjdCmZk6E
1785348476947443	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy6gI		item.create	bn-19jrt	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPR 27 (START_HERE:455) requires exposing curated native operations with explicit handles, deterministic schemas/order, and result bounds; the graph carried only the exit-evidence Bone.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nSelect the curated operation set and generate its MCP schemas from the same RFC 0026/0027 IDL the native protocol uses, so the MCP surface cannot drift from the native one. Results are ordered deterministically and bounded, with omission manifests and expansion handles reused from PR 11 rather than truncated silently.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] MCP schemas are generated from the IDL; a drift test fails if the surfaces diverge.\n- [ ] Identical requests return byte-identical, deterministically ordered results (INV-006).\n- [ ] Bounded results carry an omission manifest and expansion handle; nothing is silently truncated (INV-007).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThird-party agents must get the same honest, bounded surface as the first-party client. Generating from the IDL is what keeps 'curated' from meaning 'divergent'.\n","kind":"task","labels":["aci","implementation","protocol","req:phase-c","req:pr-27"],"parent":"bn-16v","size":"m","title":"Curated MCP operation surface with deterministic schemas, ordering, and result bounds"}	blake3:TixK2fuGL4DYvlCS3hoPCrj5EyvZELrflAaeagb13RU
1785348476947576	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy6wI		item.link	bn-183f	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-19jrt"}	blake3:NoogQuR08jlBVOQHcGOjZTnKH8u-Tm0oev8fnVTExns
