1785348476977227	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy7QI		item.link	bn-19jrt	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-3zt"}	blake3:vXpbA91gnX75LMyCAUKBzb2YrZmSJ_Gy-OqRrhbZ8ec
1785348477051609	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy7gI		item.create	bn-2n29a	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPR 27's exit criterion (START_HERE:455): two subagents share one workspace and intent but use isolated debugger and proof handles without session coupling.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nEnforce capability checks on every MCP operation independently of handle possession, and isolate debugger/proof handles per agent so one subagent cannot observe or disturb another's session. Shared workspace and intent state remains shared; derived exploration state does not. Fail closed on any unrecognized capability.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Two subagents share one workspace/intent but hold isolated debugger and proof handles with no session coupling.\n- [ ] Authorization is checked independently of handle possession (G1-07 contract) on the MCP path too.\n- [ ] An unprivileged MCP client cannot promote evidence or perform a privileged intent change; the prompt-injection corpus is run against this surface.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe MCP adapter is the most likely place for a privileged side door to appear, because it is the surface built for untrusted third-party agents. G2's no-side-door property has to be enforced here explicitly.\n","kind":"task","labels":["aci","implementation","protocol","req:phase-c","req:pr-27"],"parent":"bn-16v","size":"m","title":"MCP capability checks and per-agent handle isolation"}	blake3:zKOcWn4h_xhdSKvhMQQQ-KYtYiMHPMlyK0c-bFNZDKw
