1785348477085311	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy8QI		item.link	bn-2n29a	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-3zt"}	blake3:NqWjCd9CLd6d68lzx7XTL63dUdX_tOYq7ucqwbND7YY
1785348477157701	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy8gI		item.create	bn-2jsks	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`PR-15B-IMPL-02` was bundled onto the formatter Bone (bn-h4o8), which left the migration tool with no work item of its own even though it is separately gated by PHASE-C-DEL-07.\nSource: `notes/plan/notes/START_HERE_IMPLEMENTATION.md:309`.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nImplement the migration tool that rewrites existing CML sources across surface-syntax revisions, defined — like the formatter — over the normalized semantic AST that is frozen before surface syntax (docs/11 §14). Migration is semantics-preserving by construction: the migrated source must elaborate to the same semantic model identity.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A migrated source elaborates to the same semantic model identity as its pre-migration form (identity, not equivalence).\n- [ ] Migration is idempotent and deterministic across the replicated-register model and the Wave 0 CML ports.\n- [ ] A revision pair with no defined migration path fails loudly rather than migrating approximately.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nSurface syntax cannot be declared stable until existing sources can be carried across revisions. Same-identity elaboration is the proof that migration adds zero semantic risk.\n","kind":"task","labels":["cml","implementation","req:phase-c","req:pr-15b","req:pr-15b-impl-02"],"parent":"bn-380","size":"m","title":"PR-15B / IMPL-02 — CML migration tool rewriting sources across surface-syntax revisions"}	blake3:VQ3c2dLIFlcIue4fo0IZD06WYxC_91J8wRFv0VhUEo0
