1785348477560746	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyggM		item.create	bn-31ie2	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase A authors the seven RFCs as normative specifications; Phases B–E implement them. Documentation work is otherwise scheduled only in Phase A (spec authoring) and Phase F (user-facing docs), which leaves four phases in which the normative specifications can drift away from the code with nothing scheduled to catch it. bn-28ur establishes the dossier-sync mechanism; this Bone applies it to the normative specification surface Phase B changes.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nAs PR 14–22a land, reconcile RFC 0031 (semantic and protected-intent diff), RFC 0032 (repair transaction and promotion receipt), RFC 0038 (multi-agent evidence-graph enforcement), docs/11 (CML), and docs/41 (repair design, per SD-10) against what was actually built. Where implementation and specification disagree, the disagreement is resolved explicitly in one commit — the specification is amended or the code is fixed, never left divergent.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every Phase B PR that changes behavior specified in an RFC lands its specification amendment in the same commit.\n- [ ] RFC 0031, RFC 0032, RFC 0038, docs/11, and docs/41 describe the Phase B implementation as built, with no known divergences at the exit gate.\n- [ ] Any divergence that cannot be resolved is recorded in the specification-debt ledger with an owner, rather than left silent.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nINV-003 makes the normative specifications load-bearing machine contracts, not commentary. A specification that has quietly stopped being true is worse than no specification, because downstream adapters and generated clients are built from it.\n","kind":"task","labels":["docs","governance","quality","req:phase-b"],"parent":"bn-3zt","size":"m","title":"Normative documentation reconciliation — Phase B (RFC 0031/0032/0038, docs/11, docs/41)"}	blake3:t37DLgJgQXoHMryFR0zPjLIH-qpYJKJ6zojinE35CaM
1785348477560895	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEygwM		item.link	bn-1itt	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-31ie2"}	blake3:ZTlak8sZuJjzJXE_OHHyMexconuoC1iZwAYTA70yDf8
1785348477590645	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyhAM		item.link	bn-31ie2	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-19p"}	blake3:t8W_OqcrmTUNCN3FBBtj9TN9yAdXBG5zezv96iT1Ctc
1785348477651873	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyhQM		item.create	bn-1n0f8	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase C ships the surfaces third parties build against — LSP, DAP, SARIF, MCP, the incremental query engine — and the CML syntax-stability declaration. These are exactly the specifications an ecosystem depends on, and nothing in the graph scheduled keeping them true.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nAs PR 15b–27b land, reconcile RFC 0027 (agent tool and capability protocol), RFC 0028 (Context Pack and expansion protocol), RFC 0030 (incremental query and parity audit), the docs/34 latency table, and the published adapter schemas against the implementation. Publish and version the adapter schemas alongside the code so a generated client cannot be built from a stale contract.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] RFC 0027, RFC 0028, and RFC 0030 describe the Phase C implementation as built at the exit gate.\n- [ ] The docs/34 latency table matches the measured reference-workload numbers, or the gap is recorded as a gate finding.\n- [ ] Adapter schemas (LSP, DAP, SARIF, MCP) are published, versioned, and drift-tested against the code.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nPhase C is where outside parties start depending on Continuum's contracts. A drift-tested, versioned schema surface is what makes the Phase F ecosystem release possible without a breaking rewrite.\n","kind":"task","labels":["docs","governance","quality","req:phase-c"],"parent":"bn-1fd","size":"m","title":"Normative documentation reconciliation — Phase C (RFC 0027/0028/0030, docs/34, adapter surfaces)"}	blake3:hoTqGALiNR46vZptk7qHyEwukOqjaejhRl7TGKf4t4c
1785348477652113	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyhgM		item.link	bn-waj3	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-1n0f8"}	blake3:Zkf-sQ-oVz9TRQ68HTUnaOtblxONneZ7vIy0YbH50Mw
1785348477682717	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyhwM		item.link	bn-1n0f8	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-3zt"}	blake3:oAl2opxiuUKc9DSW8NF2q0XYP-OAB3a7WWsWvbnUXxQ
1785348477745277	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyiAM		item.create	bn-3338m	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase D introduces the proof lane: Lean proof service, theorem and certificate contracts, and the proof Context Pack. Version-explicit proof (INV-014) means the documented contract and the shipped checker have to agree exactly, and nothing scheduled that reconciliation.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nAs PR 28 and the Wave 0/1 parity raises land, reconcile RFC 0012 (theorem and certificate contract), the proof Context Pack schema, the axiom-manifest format, and the proof-obligation ledger against the implementation. Pin and document the Lean toolchain version wherever the contract is version-sensitive.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] RFC 0012 and the proof Context Pack schema describe the shipped proof service as built at the exit gate.\n- [ ] Every documented certificate and axiom-manifest format is version-explicit and matches the checker (INV-014).\n- [ ] The proof-obligation ledger reflects what the Phase D checkers actually discharge, including anything still deferred.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nA proof claim is only as good as the documented contract a reader can check it against. Version-implicit proof documentation is how a checker upgrade silently invalidates every prior certificate.\n","kind":"task","labels":["docs","governance","quality","req:phase-d"],"parent":"bn-1db","size":"m","title":"Normative documentation reconciliation — Phase D (RFC 0012, proof-service and certificate contracts)"}	blake3:VDZC_NX3JsJj0IHqWEQeahiace_3PtIXOzTUDnkvkYo
